WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.311
- Hey everyone, James Rath here.

00:00:01.311 --> 00:00:02.730
So recently I've been seeing

00:00:02.730 --> 00:00:04.290
some articles that say

00:00:04.290 --> 00:00:07.951
Facebook helps the blind see images.

00:00:07.951 --> 00:00:10.031
Now, I put quotations over see because

00:00:10.031 --> 00:00:12.331
obviously this isn't 100% accurate,

00:00:12.331 --> 00:00:14.671
these are just sort of click-baity titles.

00:00:14.671 --> 00:00:17.471
But Facebook has taken another step to

00:00:17.471 --> 00:00:19.751
sort of push accessibility for its users,

00:00:19.751 --> 00:00:21.613
which is actually really cool.

00:00:21.613 --> 00:00:24.194
But it's still not quite there yet,

00:00:24.194 --> 00:00:26.094
from what they want it to be.

00:00:26.094 --> 00:00:27.174
So this feature that I've talking about

00:00:27.174 --> 00:00:28.294
that Facebook just rolled out

00:00:28.294 --> 00:00:32.294
is allowing the voice readers in

00:00:32.294 --> 00:00:36.194
iPhone and Mac, no, not Mac,

00:00:36.194 --> 00:00:38.186
I think it's just on iOS.

00:00:38.186 --> 00:00:39.756
So this feature that Facebook rolled out

00:00:39.756 --> 00:00:42.035
is basically a addition to

00:00:42.035 --> 00:00:45.795
just allowing your voiceover
on your iPhone and iPad

00:00:45.795 --> 00:00:48.116
reading other posts and,

00:00:48.116 --> 00:00:50.636
everything that's on the
screen on the Facebook app.

00:00:50.636 --> 00:00:52.536
But now it will actually try to describe

00:00:52.536 --> 00:00:55.566
what's in the images
that your friends share

00:00:55.566 --> 00:00:58.476
that are uploaded to Facebook
by pages and what not.

00:00:58.476 --> 00:01:00.096
So the way that Facebook
is describing this feature

00:01:00.096 --> 00:01:02.175
is that artificial intelligence
will be able to sort of

00:01:02.175 --> 00:01:04.276
scan the image and tell you

00:01:04.276 --> 00:01:05.936
how many people are in the image

00:01:05.936 --> 00:01:07.796
and if there's a rainforest,

00:01:07.796 --> 00:01:10.276
if it's trees, if it's an ocean,

00:01:10.276 --> 00:01:13.216
if it's a road, what is in this image.

00:01:13.216 --> 00:01:14.956
It's not perfect, I've tried it myself.

00:01:14.956 --> 00:01:17.155
In fact, it's not even that good yet.

00:01:17.155 --> 00:01:20.875
But I went through and I
had it read all of my like

00:01:20.875 --> 00:01:22.756
Instagram pictures and some were like

00:01:22.756 --> 00:01:25.406
beaches, some were skies and

00:01:25.406 --> 00:01:26.856
it just wasn't able to recognize that,

00:01:26.856 --> 00:01:28.276
it wasn't able to recognize a dog.

00:01:28.276 --> 00:01:29.717
But it, what it was able to recognize is

00:01:29.717 --> 00:01:31.236
how many people were in an image.

00:01:31.236 --> 00:01:32.717
So that was kinda cool,

00:01:32.717 --> 00:01:34.256
but then I thought about.

00:01:34.256 --> 00:01:35.937
You know, Facebook already can tell

00:01:35.937 --> 00:01:37.596
with its artifical intelligence,

00:01:37.596 --> 00:01:39.157
how many people are in an image.

00:01:39.157 --> 00:01:41.437
What I mean by that is whenever
I'm uploading a picture to

00:01:41.437 --> 00:01:43.817
Facebook and say it's a
group of my friends and I.

00:01:43.817 --> 00:01:46.138
Image is posted, it's asking me like

00:01:46.138 --> 00:01:47.957
who do you want to tag in this image.

00:01:47.957 --> 00:01:50.018
It's already able to sort
of recognize the faces.

00:01:50.018 --> 00:01:52.037
Now not only can it
recognize what a face is but

00:01:52.037 --> 00:01:54.798
it already knows like who is Mike,

00:01:54.798 --> 00:01:56.878
and it knows who is Matt,

00:01:56.878 --> 00:01:59.338
and who's, I'm just
saying random names here.

00:01:59.338 --> 00:02:01.317
But it already knows who my friends are,

00:02:01.317 --> 00:02:02.847
based on their face and,

00:02:02.847 --> 00:02:05.648
that's a little creepy,

00:02:05.648 --> 00:02:07.398
it's cool in a way,

00:02:07.398 --> 00:02:10.819
but it's a thing and Facebook
has had that for years now.

00:02:10.819 --> 00:02:13.219
But now it just sort of narrates that.

00:02:13.219 --> 00:02:15.740
So maybe other users have had success

00:02:15.740 --> 00:02:17.320
where it's able to describe like

00:02:17.320 --> 00:02:20.680
a beach or maybe it's just the most

00:02:20.680 --> 00:02:23.940
simplistic photos that it
can sort of read objects of.

00:02:23.940 --> 00:02:27.829
But as of yet, I still
cannot get it to read

00:02:27.829 --> 00:02:30.420
any more or describe any more than just

00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:32.319
how many people are in an image.

00:02:32.319 --> 00:02:34.520
But enough talking about
it, let's go demonstrate it.

00:02:34.520 --> 00:02:37.459
So when you have the built-in
voiceover on iOS turned on,

00:02:37.459 --> 00:02:39.910
it will narrate everything
that you're doing on screen.

00:02:39.910 --> 00:02:41.880
This is specifically helpful for those

00:02:41.880 --> 00:02:44.560
who are blind or visually impaired.

00:02:44.560 --> 00:02:46.340
(iPhone voiceover)
So this is a public

00:02:46.340 --> 00:02:49.180
Facebook page here for a public figure.

00:02:49.180 --> 00:02:52.940
(iPhone English and
foreign language voiceover)

00:02:52.940 --> 00:02:54.822
Also shows you that voiceover
can change its language

00:02:54.822 --> 00:02:57.302
when it's reading different
languages, which is cool.

00:02:57.302 --> 00:02:59.402
- [iPhone] Image may
contain one person, smiling.

00:02:59.402 --> 00:03:01.342
- [James] So that's the A.I. of Facebook,

00:03:01.342 --> 00:03:04.022
being able to tell if it's
outdoors, if it's nature,

00:03:04.022 --> 00:03:05.692
so that's actually really cool.

00:03:05.692 --> 00:03:07.882
It was only able to tell one person,

00:03:07.882 --> 00:03:09.862
it wasn't able to
recognize the other face.

00:03:09.862 --> 00:03:11.521
- [iPhone] Image may contain eight people,

00:03:11.521 --> 00:03:13.941
outdoor, water, nature

00:03:13.941 --> 00:03:16.102
- [James] So with this next image here,

00:03:16.102 --> 00:03:17.642
this one was unable to recognize

00:03:17.642 --> 00:03:18.622
that it was a dog and a cat,

00:03:18.622 --> 00:03:20.242
and that it was outdoors and nature.

00:03:20.242 --> 00:03:22.362
The image description that's narrating was

00:03:22.362 --> 00:03:25.442
actually manually put in by
the uploader of the image.

00:03:25.442 --> 00:03:27.332
Facebook wasn't the only social network

00:03:27.332 --> 00:03:29.021
to extend accessibility reach,

00:03:29.021 --> 00:03:31.002
and what I mean is Twitter
has also rolled out

00:03:31.002 --> 00:03:33.802
a feature that allows
you to have more than

00:03:33.802 --> 00:03:38.182
140 characters in a tweet with an image,

00:03:38.182 --> 00:03:40.222
but you can't actually see the text.

00:03:40.222 --> 00:03:42.102
What it is is, you can add an extra

00:03:42.102 --> 00:03:44.641
420 characters I believe,

00:03:44.641 --> 00:03:46.822
and you can use that to describe

00:03:46.822 --> 00:03:48.662
what your image that you're uploading

00:03:48.662 --> 00:03:51.442
for those of us who are
visually impaired or blind.

00:03:51.442 --> 00:03:53.241
Though you can't actually read this text,

00:03:53.241 --> 00:03:55.142
it's sort of, it's an alternate text

00:03:55.142 --> 00:03:57.862
that's in the image file,

00:03:57.862 --> 00:04:00.322
I don't know, it's technical terms.

00:04:00.322 --> 00:04:02.782
Anyway, it's invisible text that is there,

00:04:02.782 --> 00:04:04.722
but the invisible text can be picked up

00:04:04.722 --> 00:04:06.722
and read by screen readers,

00:04:06.722 --> 00:04:08.382
by voiceover on iOS,

00:04:08.382 --> 00:04:10.872
and the iPad and iPhone.

00:04:10.872 --> 00:04:12.424
So, I'm gonna demonstrate for you

00:04:12.424 --> 00:04:13.664
how to upload an image

00:04:13.664 --> 00:04:15.564
and add the image description,

00:04:15.564 --> 00:04:17.464
and then how to get it to read it to you.

00:04:17.464 --> 00:04:19.424
Let's do that now.

00:04:19.424 --> 00:04:20.904
When you're in the Twitter app,

00:04:20.904 --> 00:04:25.423
you go to the gear/settings
in your profile section.

00:04:25.423 --> 00:04:28.743
And then you click on Display and sounds,

00:04:28.743 --> 00:04:31.804
and then Accessibility at the bottom.

00:04:31.804 --> 00:04:35.084
Then make sure that you
have the toggle on for

00:04:35.084 --> 00:04:37.164
Compose image descriptions.

00:04:37.164 --> 00:04:39.587
Once you add that, when you
go to compose your tweet,

00:04:39.587 --> 00:04:42.527
select the image that you
want from your library,

00:04:42.527 --> 00:04:44.507
and then make your tweet out,

00:04:44.507 --> 00:04:47.686
if you're even gonna
tweet anything with it.

00:04:47.686 --> 00:04:49.687
Now to add the description of the image

00:04:49.687 --> 00:04:51.826
you tap on add description,

00:04:51.826 --> 00:04:53.148
then you can either paste text,

00:04:53.148 --> 00:04:56.208
you can type it or use dictation.

00:04:56.208 --> 00:04:58.107
In this case I decided
to just take the text

00:04:58.107 --> 00:04:59.807
I already had from Instagram and

00:04:59.807 --> 00:05:02.868
copy and paste it for the same image.

00:05:02.868 --> 00:05:04.188
So make sure everything's alright,

00:05:04.188 --> 00:05:06.108
then you hit add.

00:05:08.278 --> 00:05:11.048
Once you make all the final
adjustments to your image,

00:05:11.048 --> 00:05:12.368
whether it's edit or what not,

00:05:12.368 --> 00:05:13.588
you just hit the send tweet,

00:05:13.588 --> 00:05:15.688
refresh and, there it is.

00:05:15.688 --> 00:05:17.278
So now when we enable voiceover.

00:05:17.278 --> 00:05:18.408
- [iPhone] Enable voiceover.

00:05:18.408 --> 00:05:19.708
- [James] Which you can do through Siri or

00:05:19.708 --> 00:05:22.572
in the settings under Accessibility.

00:05:22.572 --> 00:05:25.471
- [iPhone/Siri] @jamesrath, This is Pal.

00:05:25.471 --> 00:05:28.132
This was the very first
dog I grew up with.

00:05:28.132 --> 00:05:30.512
She passed away a few years ago.

00:05:30.512 --> 00:05:32.172
She was the sweetest.

00:05:32.172 --> 00:05:33.572
- [James] So it reads the tweet.

00:05:33.572 --> 00:05:34.871
- [iPhone/Siri] Image. Image description,

00:05:34.871 --> 00:05:37.172
a husky dog laid down in the snow.

00:05:37.172 --> 00:05:38.832
- [James] And then it
successfully reads the

00:05:38.832 --> 00:05:43.152
invisible text caption that
you added to the image.

00:05:43.152 --> 00:05:45.752
- [iPhone/Siri] Her bright
brown eyes look into the camera.

00:05:45.752 --> 00:05:47.171
- So this whole video has kinda been about

00:05:47.171 --> 00:05:49.832
image descriptions and it's
something that I'm going to try

00:05:49.832 --> 00:05:51.931
to do now on Instagram, Tumblr,

00:05:51.931 --> 00:05:53.512
for my own original work.

00:05:53.512 --> 00:05:55.372
It's something that's
important to people who have

00:05:55.372 --> 00:05:56.971
screen readers or braille readers.

00:05:56.971 --> 00:05:58.912
It's important if you
want your content to be

00:05:58.912 --> 00:06:00.272
reaching a broader audience,

00:06:00.272 --> 00:06:02.671
if you don't want to exclude people.

00:06:02.671 --> 00:06:04.951
It's good to add image descriptions,

00:06:04.951 --> 00:06:07.082
just add a sentence, two, three sentences

00:06:07.082 --> 00:06:10.272
depending on what your
context is in the image.

00:06:10.272 --> 00:06:11.232
And just sorta describe

00:06:11.232 --> 00:06:13.873
what is happening in the picture that

00:06:13.873 --> 00:06:16.072
you're trying to share with the world.

00:06:16.072 --> 00:06:18.993
So it's cool that Twitter
is now allowing that,

00:06:18.993 --> 00:06:22.113
for when you upload an
image to the iPhone app.

00:06:22.113 --> 00:06:24.013
It's also important that you add 'em on

00:06:24.013 --> 00:06:26.793
Instagram, Facebook, Tumblr,
wherever you're sharing it.

00:06:26.793 --> 00:06:28.852
There's some do's and don'ts
for image descriptions

00:06:28.852 --> 00:06:30.153
and if you just want the best practices,

00:06:30.153 --> 00:06:31.793
I'll have a link down below to

00:06:31.793 --> 00:06:33.513
get you sort of started on that

00:06:33.513 --> 00:06:34.974
because I'm still learning how to do it.

00:06:34.974 --> 00:06:36.995
I'm just trying to figure out, you know.

00:06:36.995 --> 00:06:38.855
And it does help me sort of figure out

00:06:38.855 --> 00:06:40.236
how do I describe this?

00:06:40.236 --> 00:06:42.476
Like how would I describe
what is happening here?

00:06:42.476 --> 00:06:44.616
So it's interesting, makes
me think a little bit,

00:06:44.616 --> 00:06:46.538
it's a learning experience.

00:06:46.538 --> 00:06:47.738
So that's fun.

00:06:47.738 --> 00:06:49.297
But yeah, I just kinda wanna talk about

00:06:49.297 --> 00:06:50.738
images desrciptions, what they were and

00:06:50.738 --> 00:06:52.698
how they can benefit people

00:06:52.698 --> 00:06:55.818
and why you should as a content
creator or content sharer

00:06:55.818 --> 00:06:58.058
include your image descriptions.

00:06:58.058 --> 00:06:59.118
If you haven't seen last week's video,

00:06:59.118 --> 00:07:00.958
I'm also trying to campaign for Youtube to

00:07:00.958 --> 00:07:02.599
add the ability for creators to

00:07:02.599 --> 00:07:03.829
have audio descriptions,

00:07:03.829 --> 00:07:06.159
so after you upload a
video and you want to

00:07:06.159 --> 00:07:08.719
add narrations for people
who are visually impaired,

00:07:08.719 --> 00:07:11.059
have cognitive disabilities or

00:07:11.059 --> 00:07:14.699
just wanna have extra
context to what's happening.

00:07:14.699 --> 00:07:16.919
Having that extra audio track that would

00:07:16.919 --> 00:07:20.998
allow you to describe the missing pieces

00:07:20.998 --> 00:07:22.899
that you would otherwise need to see,

00:07:22.899 --> 00:07:24.639
what's happening in the video.

00:07:24.639 --> 00:07:27.679
In order to get the context of
what's happening, basically.

00:07:27.679 --> 00:07:29.239
But, there's a whole video on that

00:07:29.239 --> 00:07:31.639
and if you wanna sort of
share and help that movement,

00:07:31.639 --> 00:07:33.439
I've already sort of emailed it to

00:07:33.439 --> 00:07:34.999
some guys at Youtube and

00:07:34.999 --> 00:07:36.200
just keep sharing it,

00:07:36.200 --> 00:07:37.919
make it a priority for them to add this.

00:07:37.919 --> 00:07:39.499
Check out that video.

